WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.725 --> 00:00:08.693
&gt;&gt;&gt; Priyanka Chopra Jonas made

00:00:08.826 --> 00:00:11.929
more than 50 bollywood films.

00:00:12.063 --> 00:00:12.930
When she decided to take on

00:00:13.064 --> 00:00:13.664
projects in the U.S., it was not

00:00:13.798 --> 00:00:16.667
an easy transition.

00:00:16.801 --> 00:00:18.169
We continue our conversation

00:00:18.302 --> 00:00:20.171
with the problem she faced as

00:00:20.304 --> 00:00:20.905
she tried to make it big in

00:00:21.038 --> 00:00:31.916
America.

00:00:32.049 --> 00:00:32.683
In 2015, you come to this

00:00:32.817 --> 00:00:33.551
country.

00:00:33.551 --> 00:00:34.418
And you become the first south

00:00:34.552 --> 00:00:35.553
Asian to star in an American

00:00:35.553 --> 00:00:36.187
television series as an FBI

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:36.921
recruit in the TV show

00:00:37.054 --> 00:00:38.122
"Quantico."

00:00:38.255 --> 00:00:40.424
Here you are.

00:00:40.558 --> 00:00:41.192
&gt;&gt; You stole classified

00:00:41.559 --> 00:00:41.926
documents from a federal

00:00:42.059 --> 00:00:44.428
building.

00:00:44.562 --> 00:00:45.162
You planted surveillance

00:00:45.563 --> 00:00:46.664
equipment in my office.

00:00:46.797 --> 00:00:47.565
Do you have any idea how much

00:00:47.565 --> 00:00:49.567
trouble you'd be in if I told

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:49.900
anyone?

00:00:50.034 --> 00:00:51.669
&gt;&gt; Tell them.

00:00:51.802 --> 00:00:52.403
Tell the entire world, for all I

00:00:52.536 --> 00:00:54.171
care.

00:00:54.305 --> 00:00:56.173
You're still lying to me.

00:00:56.307 --> 00:00:58.676
You're still manipulating me.

00:00:58.809 --> 00:01:02.179
When does it stop, Liam?

00:01:02.313 --> 00:01:05.583
I am not scared of you anymore,

00:01:05.583 --> 00:01:06.417
and I will do whatever it takes

00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:07.151
to find out the truth about my

00:01:07.585 --> 00:01:09.920
father.

00:01:10.054 --> 00:01:11.589
&gt;&gt; You were a big star in India.

00:01:11.589 --> 00:01:12.656
Why did you decide to make the

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:14.425
move to the U.S.?

00:01:14.558 --> 00:01:15.926
&gt;&gt; Well, there were, you know --

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:16.694
everybody wants to grow and

00:01:16.827 --> 00:01:18.929
evolve.

00:01:19.063 --> 00:01:19.663
I was looking to kind of spread

00:01:19.797 --> 00:01:22.433
my wings.

00:01:22.566 --> 00:01:25.169
I wanted to have the opportunity

00:01:25.603 --> 00:01:27.938
to do music for a hot second, I

00:01:28.072 --> 00:01:29.173
quickly realized I'm a better

00:01:29.607 --> 00:01:30.441
actor than a pop star.

00:01:30.574 --> 00:01:31.175
I never thought about it in

00:01:31.609 --> 00:01:32.176
India, circumstances brought me

00:01:32.309 --> 00:01:35.179
here.

00:01:35.613 --> 00:01:37.648
I kind of always sort of -- I

00:01:37.782 --> 00:01:40.684
worked for about ten years to

00:01:40.818 --> 00:01:43.154
get to a point where I was a

00:01:43.287 --> 00:01:45.156
leading lady, and I wanted to be

00:01:45.289 --> 00:01:47.625
able to look for mainstream

00:01:47.625 --> 00:01:49.660
spots in entertainment here.

00:01:49.794 --> 00:01:52.430
There were very few people like

00:01:52.563 --> 00:01:53.664
me that did that at that time.

00:01:53.798 --> 00:01:55.166
To be able to get that job after

00:01:55.299 --> 00:01:56.434
working towards it, you know --

00:01:56.567 --> 00:01:58.669
this was not written for an

00:01:58.803 --> 00:02:00.438
Indian woman.

00:02:00.571 --> 00:02:01.672
I just went in, auditioned like

00:02:01.806 --> 00:02:03.641
everybody else, and got the job.

00:02:03.641 --> 00:02:05.643
Was really validating.

00:02:05.643 --> 00:02:06.177
&gt;&gt; So you talk briefly about you

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:06.944
were going to be a pop star.

00:02:07.078 --> 00:02:07.678
We even explored that.

00:02:07.812 --> 00:02:08.646
&gt;&gt; Oh, come on.

00:02:08.779 --> 00:02:09.647
Stop it, Chris.

00:02:09.647 --> 00:02:10.414
&gt;&gt; It's not so bad, priyanka.

00:02:10.548 --> 00:02:14.919
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:02:15.052 --> 00:02:16.921
&gt;&gt; So you had a song that for a

00:02:17.054 --> 00:02:18.923
while was the opening song on

00:02:19.056 --> 00:02:19.657
NFL network Thursday night

00:02:19.723 --> 00:02:20.191
football.

00:02:20.324 --> 00:02:21.192
Here you are.

00:02:21.325 --> 00:02:23.661
♪♪

00:02:23.794 --> 00:02:25.663
♪ I know you've got your own

00:02:25.796 --> 00:02:26.931
town I know you've got your own

00:02:27.064 --> 00:02:30.167
ways ♪

00:02:30.301 --> 00:02:33.170
♪ I know there's a place like

00:02:33.304 --> 00:02:35.172
home but I know you're gonna

00:02:35.306 --> 00:02:37.174
like it ♪

00:02:37.308 --> 00:02:39.677
&gt;&gt; I was so young.

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:40.678
&gt;&gt; Is that what you do, look

00:02:40.811 --> 00:02:41.912
back at her --

00:02:42.046 --> 00:02:47.251
&gt;&gt; That was 10 years ago or

00:02:47.685 --> 00:02:47.852
something, I was 30 or

00:02:47.985 --> 00:02:49.320
something, that's crazy.

00:02:49.687 --> 00:02:52.056
&gt;&gt; I heard the reaction in

00:02:52.189 --> 00:02:52.790
social media was not -- I mean,

00:02:52.923 --> 00:02:54.058
yes there were obviously people

00:02:54.191 --> 00:02:54.792
who liked it, but also a lot of

00:02:54.925 --> 00:02:55.693
racism.

00:02:55.693 --> 00:02:57.795
&gt;&gt; Well, yeah.

00:02:57.928 --> 00:02:58.796
NFL, NFL network got a lot of

00:02:58.929 --> 00:03:00.798
the tweets and stuff.

00:03:00.931 --> 00:03:03.300
Or things saying, who's she?

00:03:03.701 --> 00:03:07.304
Who's this person?

00:03:07.705 --> 00:03:08.038
Why should she be on American

00:03:08.172 --> 00:03:08.806
TV?

00:03:08.939 --> 00:03:09.707
Football, it should be an

00:03:09.707 --> 00:03:12.042
American person.

00:03:12.176 --> 00:03:13.310
It was very confusing because I

00:03:13.711 --> 00:03:14.044
was just an artist with a song

00:03:14.178 --> 00:03:16.046
that they liked.

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:16.780
But props to NFL, they doubled

00:03:16.914 --> 00:03:19.049
down for next year.

00:03:19.183 --> 00:03:20.784
&gt;&gt; Was that hurtful to you, or

00:03:20.918 --> 00:03:22.286
by that point had you sort of

00:03:22.419 --> 00:03:23.053
been through that and it didn't

00:03:23.187 --> 00:03:24.555
hit you?

00:03:24.688 --> 00:03:25.789
&gt;&gt; No, it hits you every time.

00:03:25.923 --> 00:03:26.557
It hits you.

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:27.725
All of it hits you.

00:03:27.725 --> 00:03:29.727
Criticism.

00:03:29.727 --> 00:03:31.295
The fact that my job requires me

00:03:31.729 --> 00:03:34.798
to be dissected, and you know,

00:03:34.932 --> 00:03:38.035
microscopically -- everything I

00:03:38.169 --> 00:03:40.070
say, my home, my family,

00:03:40.204 --> 00:03:41.805
everything is looked at.

00:03:41.939 --> 00:03:43.307
But that's the nature of the

00:03:43.741 --> 00:03:44.041
beast, and I've done it for 23

00:03:44.175 --> 00:03:45.042
years now.

00:03:45.176 --> 00:03:45.809
I'm not expecting its nature to

00:03:45.943 --> 00:03:46.577
change.

00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:47.311
You've got to start protecting

00:03:47.745 --> 00:03:48.045
yourself, and I started doing

00:03:48.179 --> 00:03:48.779
that many years ago.

00:03:48.913 --> 00:03:49.780
Yes, of course it was hurtful.

00:03:49.914 --> 00:03:50.548
But I'm someone who does wear

00:03:50.681 --> 00:03:52.883
blinders.

00:03:53.017 --> 00:03:56.520
I'm not -- and I believe in, you

00:03:56.654 --> 00:03:57.788
know, letting my work speak for

00:03:57.922 --> 00:03:59.190
itself.

00:03:59.323 --> 00:04:00.691
And the best shutdown to people

00:04:00.824 --> 00:04:01.425
like that who said that I didn't

00:04:01.759 --> 00:04:02.660
belong is to belong.

00:04:02.793 --> 00:04:03.928
Today I have, you know, the

00:04:04.061 --> 00:04:05.396
number one series in the world

00:04:05.763 --> 00:04:06.430
on the show, and I'm sitting

00:04:06.564 --> 00:04:07.765
here talking to Chris Wallace.

00:04:07.798 --> 00:04:08.432
&gt;&gt; Man, it doesn't get better

00:04:08.566 --> 00:04:09.433
than that.

00:04:09.767 --> 00:04:10.701
&gt;&gt; So who won?

00:04:10.834 --> 00:04:13.170
Who won?

00:04:13.304 --> 00:04:14.171
&gt;&gt; One of the things that

00:04:14.305 --> 00:04:18.108
changed as you grew and got more

00:04:18.242 --> 00:04:18.943
success, I heard that when you

00:04:19.076 --> 00:04:21.779
started, you felt you had to

00:04:21.812 --> 00:04:25.182
shed your ethnicity to get jobs,

00:04:25.316 --> 00:04:28.919
to get accepted here in the U.S.

00:04:29.053 --> 00:04:30.421
But that as you've grown and

00:04:30.554 --> 00:04:31.422
become priyanka Chopra Jonas,

00:04:31.789 --> 00:04:32.189
that you are -- now you embrace

00:04:32.323 --> 00:04:41.398
your ethnicity on screen?

00:04:41.799 --> 00:04:43.934
&gt;&gt; When I first started working

00:04:44.068 --> 00:04:44.935
here, I think that -- I did not

00:04:45.069 --> 00:04:45.803
want to be the checking the box.

00:04:45.803 --> 00:04:46.670
Normalizing my culture has been

00:04:46.804 --> 00:04:49.807
a long walk.

00:04:49.807 --> 00:04:50.674
And there are few of us that are

00:04:50.808 --> 00:04:51.809
doing it, and culture should be

00:04:51.809 --> 00:04:53.177
normalized because children

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:53.944
should be exposed to different

00:04:54.078 --> 00:04:56.680
kinds of culture.

00:04:56.814 --> 00:04:58.415
Culture's the best teacher in

00:04:58.549 --> 00:05:01.919
the world.

00:05:02.052 --> 00:05:03.821
Traveling around the world,

00:05:03.821 --> 00:05:06.423
meeting -- different foods,

00:05:06.557 --> 00:05:09.827
different forms, languages.

00:05:09.827 --> 00:05:13.330
I think that became my quest for

00:05:13.464 --> 00:05:13.831
a very long time.

00:05:13.964 --> 00:05:14.665
I'm doing a lot of work where I

00:05:14.798 --> 00:05:16.667
want to bring stories that are

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:17.434
about my culture and my people

00:05:17.835 --> 00:05:18.435
and should be out there.

00:05:18.569 --> 00:05:19.169
&gt;&gt; Well, speaking of your

00:05:19.303 --> 00:05:19.937
culture, one way you embraced it

00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:22.840
was your wedding to pop singer

00:05:22.973 --> 00:05:24.074
Nick Jonas with this big

00:05:24.208 --> 00:05:26.043
extravaganza in jodhpur.

00:05:26.176 --> 00:05:26.810
Why did you want it to be so

00:05:26.944 --> 00:05:27.845
big?

00:05:27.945 --> 00:05:28.545
&gt;&gt; Because I never said I would

00:05:28.679 --> 00:05:31.048
settle.

00:05:31.181 --> 00:05:32.283
Everything I do is big.

00:05:32.416 --> 00:05:37.054
I'm a bold person.

00:05:37.187 --> 00:05:41.892
It was big in scale, but it was

00:05:42.026 --> 00:05:43.861
it was the closest people to me

00:05:43.861 --> 00:05:45.863
and my husband, and we kept it

00:05:45.996 --> 00:05:49.033
very intimate, but I wanted to

00:05:49.166 --> 00:05:52.503
get my head in a palace with a

00:05:52.636 --> 00:05:53.504
75-foot train.

00:05:53.871 --> 00:05:54.371
Why not?

00:05:54.505 --> 00:05:55.472
Why not?

00:05:55.873 --> 00:05:55.973
&gt;&gt; And you could.

00:05:56.106 --> 00:05:57.474
&gt;&gt; And I did.

00:05:57.875 --> 00:05:58.008
&gt;&gt; All right.

00:05:58.142 --> 00:06:00.811
Then last year, you and Nick had

00:06:00.944 --> 00:06:05.883
your baby girl who was born

00:06:06.016 --> 00:06:07.885
dangerously premature.

00:06:07.951 --> 00:06:10.888
How big was she when she was

00:06:11.021 --> 00:06:11.188
born?

00:06:11.322 --> 00:06:11.989
&gt;&gt; Very small.

00:06:12.122 --> 00:06:13.891
She was very, very small.

00:06:13.891 --> 00:06:15.893
It was very touch and go for

00:06:16.026 --> 00:06:18.362
about her whole third trimester.

00:06:18.495 --> 00:06:23.500
She was in the NICU, and it was

00:06:23.901 --> 00:06:25.102
many, many scary moments, but my

00:06:25.235 --> 00:06:26.704
husband and I had each other,

00:06:26.837 --> 00:06:28.572
and we had to be really tough in

00:06:28.706 --> 00:06:29.473
that situation because she

00:06:29.907 --> 00:06:30.374
needed that.

00:06:30.507 --> 00:06:34.144
And here she is, happy as a

00:06:34.278 --> 00:06:37.047
clam, smileyest Bailey no.

00:06:37.181 --> 00:06:40.818
&gt;&gt; So she had to spend 100 days

00:06:40.951 --> 00:06:44.321
in icu, and as I understand it

00:06:44.455 --> 00:06:45.989
when she came home and you

00:06:46.123 --> 00:06:48.092
didn't have all the monitors and

00:06:48.225 --> 00:06:48.926
nurses that all the time you

00:06:49.059 --> 00:06:53.030
would be putting your ear to her

00:06:53.163 --> 00:06:53.931
heart --

00:06:53.997 --> 00:06:56.100
&gt;&gt; Or my finger in front of her

00:06:56.233 --> 00:06:58.736
knows to make sure she was

00:06:58.869 --> 00:06:59.103
breathing.

00:06:59.236 --> 00:07:02.306
Because I was a NICU mom.

00:07:02.439 --> 00:07:04.508
The monitor didn't know what she

00:07:04.641 --> 00:07:06.043
was feeling.

00:07:06.176 --> 00:07:07.945
She couldn't try or communicate

00:07:07.945 --> 00:07:09.346
in any way.

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:11.982
She had so many tubes in her,

00:07:12.116 --> 00:07:14.218
you could hardly see her face.

00:07:14.351 --> 00:07:16.720
It was very terrifying, but we

00:07:16.854 --> 00:07:18.455
had so much support from our

00:07:18.589 --> 00:07:18.789
family.

00:07:18.922 --> 00:07:20.524
My mom, my husband's parents

00:07:20.657 --> 00:07:22.659
came down and helped us, helped

00:07:22.793 --> 00:07:24.695
her be surrounded by love and

00:07:24.828 --> 00:07:26.430
feel cherished.

00:07:26.563 --> 00:07:30.734
She's the happiest little thing.

00:07:30.868 --> 00:07:32.870
&gt;&gt; How's she doing now?

00:07:33.003 --> 00:07:33.971
&gt;&gt; She's great.

00:07:34.071 --> 00:07:35.072
She's almost walking.

00:07:35.205 --> 00:07:36.140
We're just about there now.

00:07:36.273 --> 00:07:38.108
&gt;&gt; Finally, hold up your

00:07:38.242 --> 00:07:40.144
right -- because I kept noticing

00:07:40.277 --> 00:07:40.444
this.

00:07:40.577 --> 00:07:42.379
Tell me about that and the story

00:07:42.513 --> 00:07:43.080
behind it.

00:07:43.213 --> 00:07:46.717
&gt;&gt; Well, it says daddy's little

00:07:46.850 --> 00:07:48.552
girl, and it's my dad's hand

00:07:48.685 --> 00:07:48.886
writing.

00:07:49.019 --> 00:07:52.389
I lost my dad to cancer in 2013,

00:07:52.523 --> 00:07:55.125
and he was my greatest champion,

00:07:55.259 --> 00:07:58.595
biggest fan, like gregarious

00:07:58.729 --> 00:08:00.731
laugh, loudest in the room, just

00:08:00.864 --> 00:08:02.900
confident guy, and there was a

00:08:03.033 --> 00:08:05.002
lot of silence after I lost my

00:08:05.135 --> 00:08:08.539
dad in my life.

00:08:08.672 --> 00:08:11.308
But he would never let me get a

00:08:11.442 --> 00:08:12.843
tattoo, so I thought if I

